Ostra Studio Website Copy
Oct 20, 2026 · @Precious
Voice and rules
Every page speaks to one person: a homeowner or developer with a real site, a real budget and a real worry about getting it wrong. Copy answers that worry first, then shows the work. Text in [square brackets] is a placeholder for a fact the client has not given us; nothing in brackets is approved to publish.
Audience. Private homeowners planning a new build or major remodel (primary), small developers and landowners (secondary), and press, recruits and suppliers (tertiary). Their words in this deck are drafted from the brief, not from real client calls, so they need checking against inquiry emails, call notes and reviews before launch.
Primary action. Book a consultation. One button label everywhere: "Book a consultation".
Rule
In practice
Benefit before feature
Lead with what changes for the client (light, calm, a build that stays on budget), then how Ostra does it
Headlines
5 to 7 words, sentence case, one idea, no full stop unless it is a statement of belief
Subtext
Two lines at most, plain words, names the project type and the outcome
Voice
Calm, exact, a little dry. First person plural ("we") for the studio, "you" for the client
Words to avoid
"passionate", "world-class", "bespoke" (unless proved), "seamless", "vision" used alone, "solutions"
Proof
Only named projects, real numbers and real quotes. Unverified items stay in brackets
Buttons
Verb + object: "Book a consultation", "See the work", "Read the case study"
Scannability
Subheads every 2 to 3 short paragraphs; bullets for parallel items; one idea per paragraph
Formulas used. Each page names its goal and its formula so the writer can see why it is built that way.
• ABC (attention, benefit, call): hero and short intros.
• PAS (problem, agitate, solve): the consultation CTA and the Contact page.
• BAB (before, after, bridge): the philosophy block and the Approach page.
Hero formula. [Descriptive word] + [what we do] for [who] + [main benefit].
Home
Goal: get a qualified homeowner to book a consultation. Formulas: hero = ABC, philosophy = BAB, closing CTA = PAS.
Hero
• Headline: Modern homes shaped around your life.
• Alternates: Houses that hold the light. · Custom homes, drawn before they're built.
• Subtext: Ostra Studio designs custom houses and major remodels, from first sketch to handover. [Confirm design through delivery.]
• Primary button: Book a consultation
• Secondary link: See the work
• Trust line under the buttons: [Years in practice] years. [Number] homes built. [Region or regions served].
Philosophy (before, after, bridge)
Heading: A house should fit the way you live.
Most homes are built for an average family that does not exist. Rooms end up too dark, too loud or too far from each other. You feel it every morning.
A house drawn around your routines feels different. Light lands where you sit. Rooms open when you want company and close when you don't.
We get there by starting with how you live, not with a floor plan. Then we draw, test and refine until the house is ready to build.
Link: How we work
Flagship work intro
Heading: Recent work
Subtext: Homes we designed and saw built. Each one started with a conversation like the one you are about to have.
Card pattern: [Project name] · [Location] · [Type, e.g. New build] · [Year]
Button: See all projects
Testimonials frame
Heading: What clients say
Quote slot: "[Verbatim client quote about the result, not about the studio.]" [Client first name, project, location, year]
Use two to three quotes, each on a different concern: the design, the process, the budget. No quote goes live without written permission.
Closing CTA (problem, agitate, solve)
Heading: Build it right the first time.
Body: A home is the biggest decision most people make, and fixing a mistake after the walls go up costs time and money. A short call with us gets your brief, budget and site on the table before anything is drawn.
Button: Book a consultation
Reassurance under the button: [Free or paid] 30-minute call. No obligation. We reply within [one working day].
Work index and by-type pages
Goal: let a visitor find a project like theirs in under 30 seconds and see the studio can handle it. Formula: ABC.
Work index
• Headline: Homes we've designed and built.
• Subtext: Browse by project type or location. Every project links to the full story, including the budget range and what we'd do differently.
• Filter label: Show me
• Filter chips: All · New builds · Remodels · Extensions · [Other types the client confirms]
• Card pattern: [Project name] · [Location] · [Type] · [Year]
• Empty filter result: No projects match yet. Clear the filter, or tell us what you're planning and we'll show you the closest work.
• End-of-page CTA: Planning something similar? Book a consultation
Work-by-type pages
One template, filled per type. Example for new builds:
• Headline: New homes, drawn from scratch.
• Intro: Building on an empty plot means every decision is yours, and every decision has a cost. These are the new builds we designed, with the choices that shaped each one. [Confirm count of new builds.]
• Block, "What to expect": three bullets on the typical path for this type: the first conversation, the design phase, the build. [Typical timelines to be confirmed.]
• Block, "Typical budget": [Range, only if the client agrees to publish it.] Helps the right inquiries arrive and the wrong ones stay away.
• CTA: Planning a new build? Book a consultation
Repeat for Remodels ("Remodels that change how a house lives.") and Extensions ("Extensions that feel like they were always there.").
Case study template
Goal: prove the studio solves the visitor's kind of problem, then move them to a consultation. Formula: before, after, bridge, told as a short story. Every case study follows the same order so visitors can scan it.
Block
Copy pattern
Title
[Project name]: [one-line result]. Example shape: "Hillside House: a family home that follows the slope."
Fact strip
Location · Type · Size [m² or sq ft] · Year · Budget range [if approved] · Role (design only, or design and delivery)
The brief
Two sentences in the client's words: who they are, what wasn't working. "[Client] came to us with a [site or house] that [problem]."
The challenge
One paragraph on the hardest constraint (slope, planning limits, budget, a listed building).
What we did
Three short paragraphs under subheads: Design, Materials, Build. Each ends with the effect on how the house feels.
The result
One paragraph with numbers where real: [months from first sketch to handover], [final cost against budget], [energy or comfort measure].
Client quote
"[Verbatim quote.]" [Name, year]
Credits
Photographer, structural engineer, builder, landscape. [Confirm names.]
Gallery
Alt text pattern: "[Room or view] of [Project name], [one concrete detail]."
Next project
Next: [Project name]
CTA
Heading: Have a site like this? Body: Tell us about it on a short call. Button: Book a consultation
Rule for numbers: if a figure isn't confirmed by the client in writing, it stays in brackets.
Studio
Goal: make the visitor trust the people behind the drawings. Formula: ABC, with a human lead.
• Headline: A small studio with a clear point of view.
• Subtext: Ostra is [number] architects and designers in [city], working on [number] projects at a time so each one gets senior attention. [Confirm team size.]
Principles
Three short statements, each with one line of proof.
1. Start with how you live. Every design begins with your routines, not a template.
2. Say the cost early. We talk budget in the first call, not after the drawings.
3. Draw it until it's right. We model and test before anything is built. [Add a real example.]
Partners and team
• Heading: The people you'll work with
• Bio frame (60 words max): [Name], [role]. [Years] years designing [type of work]. Trained at [school]. Led [named project]. Outside the studio: [one human detail].
• Photo alt text: [Name], [role] at Ostra Studio.
Credentials
[Registration body and number]. [Insurance or professional memberships.] Only list what the client can document.
Closing CTA
Heading: Meet us before you commit. Body: A first call costs you [30 minutes] and tells you if we're the right fit. Button: Book a consultation
Approach and Sustainability
Approach
Goal: remove the fear of an unknown process and set realistic expectations. Formula: BAB.
• Headline: From first sketch to handover.
• Before: Building a home feels like handing over a lot of money and hoping.
• After: You know what happens next, what it costs and who is responsible, at every stage.
• Bridge: Five stages, each with a clear output.
Stage
What you get
Typical length
1. Consultation
A frank view on your site, brief and budget
[30 minutes]
2. Concept
Sketches and a first cost range
[weeks]
3. Design
Full drawings, materials and planning submission
[months]
4. Build
Site visits, contractor oversight, regular updates
[months]
5. Handover
A finished house, a guide to using it, a walk-through
[days]
CTA: Ready for stage one? Book a consultation
Sustainability
Goal: show that environmental care is part of the design, not a label. Formula: ABC.
• Headline: Homes that cost less to live in.
• Subtext: Good design cuts energy use before any technology is added: orientation, insulation, daylight and materials that last.
• Proof slots: [Energy rating or measured result on a named project.] [Materials policy.] [Certification, if held.]
• Rule: no claim without a measured number or a named certification.
Awards, press and Careers
Awards and press
Goal: give press and prospective clients third-party proof. Formula: ABC.
• Headline: Recognised by people who know buildings.
• Subtext: Awards and coverage of our work. [Only items the client can document.]
• Entry pattern: [Publication or award] · [Project] · [Year] · [Link]
• Press contact line: Writing about us? Email [press address] for images and a project summary.
• If there is nothing yet: hide the page and its nav item rather than show an empty list.
Careers index
Goal: attract a few strong applicants, not many weak ones. Formula: ABC.
• Headline: Work on homes people live in.
• Subtext: We're a small studio, so every role carries real responsibility. [Confirm culture points.]
• Why join: three bullets, each a fact: [project mix], [mentoring or learning budget], [working pattern].
• No open roles: We're not hiring right now. Send a portfolio to [careers address] and we'll keep it on file.
